    {
      "id": "cell-32",
      "source": "<h2 id=\"approach-2-adding-regularization-and-validation-set\">Approach 2: Adding regularization and validation set</h2>\n<p>In the case of a Least Square fit, the function we are minimizing corresponds to the sum of squared difference between the observation and the predictions of our model:</p>\n<p>\\( \\sum_i (y_i-f(\\pmb X_i,\\pmb{\\beta}))^2 \\)</p>\n<p>with \\(y\\) the target vector, \\(\\pmb X\\) the input feature matrix and \\(f\\) our model</p>\n<p><strong>Regularization</strong> is a way to reduce overfitting. In the case of the linear model\nwe do so by adding to this function a <strong>penalization term which depends on coefficient weights</strong>.\nIn brief, the stronger the coefficient, the higher the penalization. So only coefficients which bring more fit than penalization will be kept.</p>\n<p>The formulas used in <code style=\"color: inherit\">scikit-learn</code> functions -other libraries may have a different parameterization, but the concepts stay the same- are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>L1 regularization</strong> (Lasso): \\(\\frac{1}{2n}\\sum_i (y_i-f(\\pmb X_i,\\pmb{\\beta}))^2 + \\alpha\\sum_{j}\\lvert\\beta_{j}\\rvert\\), with  \\(\\alpha\\) being the weight that you put on that regularization</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>L2 regularization</strong> (Ridge): \\(\\sum_i (y_i-f(\\pmb X_i,\\pmb{\\beta}))^2 + \\alpha\\sum_{j}\\beta_{j}^{2}\\)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Elastic Net</strong>: \\(\\frac{1}{2n}\\sum_i (y_i-f(\\pmb X_i,\\pmb{\\beta}))^2 + \\alpha\\sum_{j}(\\rho\\lvert\\beta_{j}\\rvert+\\frac{(1-\\rho)}{2}\\beta_{j}^{2})\\)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote class=\"comment\" style=\"border: 2px solid #ffecc1; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title comment-title\" id=\"comment-1\"><i class=\"far fa-comment-dots\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> Comment</div>\n<p>For a deeper understanding of those notions, you may look at :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.datacamp.com/community/tutorials/tutorial-ridge-lasso-elastic-net\">DataCamp’s “Regularization in R Tutorial: Ridge, Lasso and Elastic Net”</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://towardsdatascience.com/regularization-in-machine-learning-76441ddcf99a\">towards data science’s “Understanding l1 and l2 Regularization”</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote class=\"comment\" style=\"border: 2px solid #ffecc1; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title comment-title\" id=\"comment-2\"><i class=\"far fa-comment-dots\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> Comment</div>\n<p>Regularization generalize to maximum likelihood contexts as well</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Let’s try that on our data.</p>\n<p>We start by importing the <code style=\"color: inherit\">SGDRegressor</code> class from the <code style=\"color: inherit\">linear_model</code> module of the scikit-learn library. SGDRegressor stands for Stochastic Gradient Descent Regressor. It is an implementation of linear regression that uses Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) to fit the model. SGD is an optimization algorithm that updates the model parameters iteratively based on one sample at a time (or a mini-batch of samples), making it efficient for large datasets. It supports various types of regularization (L1, L2, Elastic Net) to prevent overfitting.</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-33",
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.linear_model import SGDRegressor"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "This tutorial is designed to equip you with essential knowledge and skills in Machine Learning, setting a strong foundation for exploring advanced topics such as Deep Learning."
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-34",
      "source": "<p>We would like now to perform a grid search over 50 different values – logarithmically spaced between \\(10^{−2}\\) and \\(10^2\\) – of the regularization parameter <code style=\"color: inherit\">alpha</code> for an SGDRegressor with L1 penalty (Lasso regression). The results will be stored in a DataFrame for further analysis.</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-35",
      "source": [
        "%%time\n",
        "\n",
        "logalphas = []\n",
        "\n",
        "coef_dict = {\n",
        "    \"name\": [],\n",
        "    \"coefficient\": [],\n",
        "    \"log-alpha\": [],\n",
        "}\n",
        "r2 = []\n",
        "\n",
        "for alpha in np.logspace(-2, 2, 50):\n",
        "    reg = SGDRegressor(penalty=\"l1\", alpha=alpha)\n",
        "    reg.fit(X , y)\n",
        "\n",
        "    logalphas.append(np.log10(alpha))\n",
        "    r2.append(r2_score(y, reg.predict(X)))\n",
        "\n",
        "    coef_dict[\"name\"] += list(X.columns)\n",
        "    coef_dict[\"coefficient\"] += list(reg.coef_)\n",
        "    coef_dict[\"log-alpha\"] += [np.log10(alpha)]* len(X.columns)\n",
        "\n",
        "coef_df = pd.DataFrame(coef_dict)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "This tutorial is designed to equip you with essential knowledge and skills in Machine Learning, setting a strong foundation for exploring advanced topics such as Deep Learning."
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-36",
      "source": "<p>Let’s visualize:</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-37",
      "source": [
        "fig,ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14,7))\n",
        "\n",
        "ax[0].plot(logalphas, r2)\n",
        "ax[0].set_xlabel(\"log10( alpha )\")\n",
        "ax[0].set_ylabel(\"R2\")\n",
        "\n",
        "sns.lineplot(\n",
        "    x=\"log-alpha\",\n",
        "    y=\"coefficient\",\n",
        "    hue=\"name\",\n",
        "    data=coef_df,\n",
        "    ax=ax[1],\n",
        "    legend = False,\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "fig.suptitle(\"Regression of potato data with an L1 regularization.\")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
