          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-108",
      "source": "<p>We start by splitting our data in a train and a test set</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-109",
      "source": [
        "X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"train set size:\", len(y_train))\n",
        "print(\" test set size:\", len(y_test))"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "This tutorial is designed to equip you with essential knowledge and skills in Machine Learning, setting a strong foundation for exploring advanced topics such as Deep Learning."
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-110",
      "source": "<blockquote class=\"question\" style=\"border: 2px solid #8A9AD0; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title question-title\" id=\"question-35\"><i class=\"far fa-question-circle\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> Question</div>\n<ol>\n<li>What is the size of the train set?</li>\n<li>What is the size of the test set?</li>\n</ol>\n<br/><details style=\"border: 2px solid #B8C3EA; margin: 1em 0.2em;padding: 0.5em; cursor: pointer;\"><summary>👁 View solution</summary>\n<div class=\"box-title solution-title\" id=\"solution-41\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button solution\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"solution-41\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"far fa-eye\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Solution</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<ol>\n<li>68</li>\n<li>18</li>\n</ol>\n</details>\n</blockquote>\n<p>When it comes to criterion to evaluate the quality of a split and determine the best way to split the data at each node in the tree, we can choose:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Square error</strong> (<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">\"squared_error\"</code>, default): This criterion uses the mean squared error (MSE) to evaluate splits. It minimizes the L2 loss by using the mean of the target values in each terminal node. The goal is to reduce the variance within the nodes.</li>\n<li><strong>Friedman MSE</strong> (<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">\"friedman_mse\"</code>): This criterion also uses mean squared error but incorporates Friedman’s improvement score to evaluate potential splits. It aims to improve the traditional MSE by considering the reduction in impurity more effectively.</li>\n<li><strong>Absolute error</strong> (<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">\"absolute_error\"</code>): This criterion uses the mean absolute error (MAE) to evaluate splits. It minimizes the L1 loss by using the median of the target values in each terminal node. This approach is less sensitive to outliers compared to squared error.</li>\n<li><strong>Poisson</strong> (<code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">\"poisson\"</code>): This criterion uses the reduction in Poisson deviance to evaluate splits. It is particularly useful for count data or when the target variable follows a Poisson distribution.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let’s try squared error and absolute error for the Grid Search:</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-111",
      "source": [
        "%%time\n",
        "from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\n",
        "\n",
        "grid_values = {\n",
        "    \"criterion\": [\"squared_error\" , \"absolute_error\"],\n",
        "    \"n_estimators\":[500],\n",
        "    \"max_depth\":[10,15,20],\n",
        "    \"min_samples_split\":np.arange(2,12,2)\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "grid_RF = GridSearchCV(\n",
        "    RandomForestRegressor(),\n",
        "    param_grid = grid_values,\n",
        "    scoring=\"r2\",\n",
        "    n_jobs=-1,\n",
        "    cv=5,\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "grid_RF.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Grid best score (r2): {grid_RF.best_score_:3f}\")\n",
        "print(\"Grid best parameter (max. r2): \")\n",
        "\n",
        "for k , v in grid_RF.best_params_.items():\n",
        "    print(f\"{k:>20} : {v}\")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "This tutorial is designed to equip you with essential knowledge and skills in Machine Learning, setting a strong foundation for exploring advanced topics such as Deep Learning."
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-112",
      "source": "<blockquote class=\"question\" style=\"border: 2px solid #8A9AD0; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title question-title\" id=\"question-36\"><i class=\"far fa-question-circle\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> Question</div>\n<ol>\n<li>What is the grid best score (accuracy)?</li>\n<li>Is the score better than with linear regression?</li>\n<li>What are the grid best parameter?</li>\n</ol>\n<br/><details style=\"border: 2px solid #B8C3EA; margin: 1em 0.2em;padding: 0.5em; cursor: pointer;\"><summary>👁 View solution</summary>\n<div class=\"box-title solution-title\" id=\"solution-42\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button solution\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"solution-42\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"far fa-eye\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Solution</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<ol>\n<li>0.528490</li>\n<li>With linear regression, the accuracy was 0.667</li>\n<li>Grid best parameter\n<ul>\n<li>criterion: absolute error</li>\n<li>max depth: 10</li>\n<li>min samples split: 2</li>\n<li>number of estimators: 500</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</details>\n</blockquote>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-113",
      "source": [
        "print(f\"Grid best parameter (max. r2) model on test: {grid_RF.score(\n",
        "    X_test,\n",
        "    y_test,\n",
        "):.3f}\")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "This tutorial is designed to equip you with essential knowledge and skills in Machine Learning, setting a strong foundation for exploring advanced topics such as Deep Learning."
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-114",
      "source": "<blockquote class=\"question\" style=\"border: 2px solid #8A9AD0; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title question-title\" id=\"question-37\"><i class=\"far fa-question-circle\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> Question</div>\n<ol>\n<li>What is <code style=\"color: inherit\">grid_RF.score(X_test, y_test)</code> doing?</li>\n<li>How can we interpret the values?</li>\n<li>What is the grid best parameter model on test? How do we interpret it?</li>\n</ol>\n<br/><details style=\"border: 2px solid #B8C3EA; margin: 1em 0.2em;padding: 0.5em; cursor: pointer;\"><summary>👁 View solution</summary>\n<div class=\"box-title solution-title\" id=\"solution-43\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button solution\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"solution-43\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"far fa-eye\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Solution</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<ol>\n<li>It computes and returns the \\(R^{2}\\) score of the best Random Forest Regressor model (found during the grid search) on the test dataset, providing an indication of how well the model generalizes to unseen data.</li>\n<li>An \\(R^{2}\\) score of 1 indicates perfect prediction, while a score of 0 indicates that the model does not explain any of the variability of the response data around its mean. Negative values indicate that the model performs worse than a horizontal line (mean prediction).</li>\n<li>0.613. An \\(R^{2}\\) of 0.613 means that approximately 61.3% of the variability in the target variable is explained by the model. It indicates a moderate fit. It suggests that the model captures a significant portion of the variance in the data, but there is still room for improvement.</li>\n</ol>\n</details>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Let’s now look at the feature, i.e. genes, importance:</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-115",
      "source": [
        "feature_importance = grid_RF.best_estimator_.feature_importances_\n",
        "\n",
        "sorted_features = sorted(\n",
        "    [[X_train.columns[i], abs(feature_importance[i])] for i in range(len(feature_importance))],\n",
        "    key=lambda x : x[1],\n",
        "    reverse=True,\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Features sorted per importance in discriminative process\")\n",
        "for f, w in sorted_features:\n",
        "    print(f\"{f:>20}\\t{w:.3f}\")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
