    },
    {
      "id": "cell-8",
      "source": "<hr />\n<p>We can now import the two libraries that we will be using, <strong>scanpy</strong> is the primary library that we will use and will handle all the plotting and data processing. Meanwhile, <strong>pandas</strong> is used briefly for some manual data manipulation.</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-9",
      "source": [
        "import scanpy as sc\n",
        "import pandas as pd"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-10",
      "source": "<h1 id=\"load-data\">Load Data</h1>\n<p>You can import files from your Galaxy history directly using the following code. This will depend on what number in your history the final annotated object is. If your object is dataset #1 in your history, then you import it with the following:</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-11",
      "source": [
        "mito_counted_anndata = get(1)                   # get an object from Galaxy history\n",
        "adata = sc.read_h5ad(mito_counted_anndata)      # read in the file as h5ad object"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-12",
      "source": "<p>Alternatively, if you don’t want to get the dataset from your Galaxy history, you can also download the input file from Zenodo, running the code below:</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-13",
      "source": [
        "%%bash\n",
        "wget -nv https://zenodo.org/record/7053673/files/Mito-counted_AnnData"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-14",
      "source": "\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-15",
      "source": [
        "adata = sc.read_h5ad(\"Mito-counted_AnnData\")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-16",
      "source": "<h1 id=\"filtering\">Filtering</h1>\n<p>You have generated an annotated AnnData object from your raw scRNA-seq fastq files. However, you have only completed a ‘rough’ filter of your dataset - there will still be a number of ‘cells’ that are actually just background from empty droplets or simply low-quality. There will also be genes that could be sequencing artifacts or that appear with such low frequency that statistical tools will fail to analyse them. This background garbage of both cells and genes not only makes it harder to distinguish real biological information from the noise, but also makes it computationally heavy to analyse. These spurious reads take a lot of computational power to analyse! First on our agenda is to filter this matrix to give us cleaner data to extract meaningful insight from, and to allow faster analysis.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"question\" style=\"border: 2px solid #8A9AD0; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title question-title\" id=\"question\"><i class=\"far fa-question-circle\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> Question</div>\n<ol>\n<li>What information is stored in your AnnData object? The last tool to generate this object counted the mitochondrial associated genes in your matrix. Where is that data stored?</li>\n<li>While you are figuring that out, how many genes and cells are in your object?</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\" style=\"border: 2px solid #FFE19E; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title tip-title\" id=\"tip-hint\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button tip\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"tip-hint\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"far fa-lightbulb\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Tip: Hint</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<p>Inspect the Anndata object by printing it with:</p>\n<div class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\"><div><pre style=\"color: inherit; background: transparent\"><code style=\"color: inherit\">print(adata)\n\nprint(adata.obs)\n\nprint(adata.var)\n</code></pre></div>    </div>\n</blockquote>\n<br/><details style=\"border: 2px solid #B8C3EA; margin: 1em 0.2em;padding: 0.5em; cursor: pointer;\"><summary>👁 View solution</summary>\n<div class=\"box-title solution-title\" id=\"solution\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button solution\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"solution\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"far fa-eye\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Solution</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<ol>\n<li>If you examine your AnnData object, you’ll find a number of different quality control metrics for both cells (<strong>obs</strong>) and genes (<strong>var</strong>).\n<ul>\n<li>For instance, you can see a <code style=\"color: inherit\">n_cells</code> under <strong>var</strong>, which counts the number of cells that gene appears in.</li>\n<li>In the <strong>obs</strong>, you have both discrete and log-based metrics for <code style=\"color: inherit\">n_genes</code>, how many genes are counted in a cell, and <code style=\"color: inherit\">n_counts</code>, how many UMIs are counted per cell. So, for instance, you might count multiple GAPDHs in a cell. Your <code style=\"color: inherit\">n_counts</code> should thus be higher than <code style=\"color: inherit\">n_genes</code>.</li>\n<li>But what about the mitochondria?? Within the cells information <strong>obs</strong>, the <code style=\"color: inherit\">total_counts_mito</code>,  <code style=\"color: inherit\">log1p_total_counts_mito</code>, and <code style=\"color: inherit\">pct_counts_mito</code> has been calculated for each cell.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>You can see by printing the object that the matrix is <code style=\"color: inherit\">31178 x 35734</code>. This is <code style=\"color: inherit\">obs x vars</code>, or rather, <code style=\"color: inherit\">cells x genes</code>, so there are <code style=\"color: inherit\">31178 cells</code> and <code style=\"color: inherit\">35734 genes</code> in the matrix.</li>\n</ol>\n</details>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"generate-qc-plots\">Generate QC Plots</h2>\n<p>We want to filter our cells, but first we need to know what our data looks like. There are a number of subjective choices to make within scRNA-seq analysis, for instance we now need to make our best informed decisions about where to set our thresholds (more on that soon!). We’re going to plot our data a few different ways. Different bioinformaticians might prefer to see the data in different ways, and here we are only generating some of the myriad of plots you can use. Ultimately you need to go with what makes the most sense to you.</p>\n<h2 id=\"creating-the-plots\">Creating the Plots</h2>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-17",
      "source": [
        "# Violin - genotype - log\n",
        "sc.pl.violin(\n",
        "  adata,\n",
        "  keys=['log1p_total_counts', 'log1p_n_genes_by_counts', 'pct_counts_mito'],\n",
        "  groupby='genotype',\n",
        "  save='-genotype-log.png'\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-18",
      "source": "\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-19",
      "source": [
        "# Violin - sex - log\n",
        "sc.pl.violin(\n",
        "  adata,\n",
        "  keys=['log1p_total_counts', 'log1p_n_genes_by_counts', 'pct_counts_mito'],\n",
        "  groupby='sex',\n",
        "  save='-sex-log.png'\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-20",
      "source": "\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-21",
      "source": [
        "# Violin - batch - log\n",
        "sc.pl.violin(\n",
        "  adata,\n",
        "  keys=['log1p_total_counts', 'log1p_n_genes_by_counts', 'pct_counts_mito'],\n",
        "  groupby='batch',\n",
        "  save='-batch-log.png'\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-22",
      "source": "\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-23",
      "source": [
        "# Scatter - mito x UMIs\n",
        "sc.pl.scatter(\n",
        "  adata,\n",
        "  x='log1p_total_counts',\n",
        "  y='pct_counts_mito',\n",
        "  save='-mitoxUMIs.png'\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-24",
      "source": "\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-25",
      "source": [
        "# Scatter - mito x genes\n",
        "sc.pl.scatter(\n",
        "  adata,\n",
        "  x='log1p_n_genes_by_counts',\n",
        "  y='pct_counts_mito',\n",
        "  save='-mitoxgenes.png'\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-26",
      "source": "\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-27",
      "source": [
        "# Scatter - genes x UMIs\n",
        "sc.pl.scatter(\n",
        "  adata,\n",
        "  x='log1p_total_counts',\n",
        "  y='log1p_n_genes_by_counts',\n",
        "  color='pct_counts_mito',\n",
        "  save='-genesxUMIs.png'\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-28",
      "source": "<h2 id=\"analysing-the-plots\">Analysing the plots</h2>\n<p>That’s a lot of information! Let’s attack this in sections and see what questions these plots can help us answer.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"question\" style=\"border: 2px solid #8A9AD0; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title question-title\" id=\"question-batch-variation\"><i class=\"far fa-question-circle\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> Question: Batch Variation</div>\n<p>Are there differences in sequencing depth across the samples?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Which plot(s) addresses this?</li>\n<li>How do you interpret it?</li>\n</ol>\n<br/><details style=\"border: 2px solid #B8C3EA; margin: 1em 0.2em;padding: 0.5em; cursor: pointer;\"><summary>👁 View solution</summary>\n<div class=\"box-title solution-title\" id=\"solution-1\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button solution\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"solution-1\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"far fa-eye\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Solution</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<ol>\n<li>The plot <code style=\"color: inherit\">violin - batch - log</code> will have what you’re looking for!\n<figure id=\"figure-1\" style=\"max-width: 90%;\"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAABTsAAAD/CAMAAAAJ1fJhAAAAclBMVEX///+C\nW1T6+/pWUFdCOz0PDQ2RdK4DAQL//vwyMjI7kjsnIyPSh7wmDAkyc6C9PT25\nubjhgSuDf4Xs7OxtZmn29vXe3d2hoaEqRjhvRjsIHRrPzs5YLR+UkZE6W0+k\nT0ZAbItKhkrRhkSCc3G6f06re5zERFXmAAAACXBIWXMAAA7zAAAO8wEcU5k6\nAAAgAElEQVR42uxdDYPatrYESQ5SFHpVS7YBYzYJ2///F98cyTY22Ebat2y3\nG3hNetMX1mBZo/MxZ2a1SnjJVbF6vh78Yl/yUs/Xc0X/6Fssn3fu8a/nTX6+\nnq+vh57Pff0xC/MMH55H4TPs/Kw7VD5X5pl4PV9PmH6+PuT1LHZ+0AZ4gufz\n9Xx9nUPqCZyfOSV4BinPFx6d5yb9pC/7vAUfEtx/XMXzuaJf6y7L54p+ziW1\nSqvn6+GvrMAW+JigsNBaPG/4o19aFx8Gney5RT9kSVUxn81N7l31JxQy/vVU\nVn1EJ0r+MSv6CSoU6gN7i3/Oin7QI8NSlpSxP3RdpPwURV31Ra/1L6Mm+2or\n2p8Gkg3+8MTO97zFLHVJ2dx5rZ4N7v/mTpOjxf0zoxT5VU9D+cedhquPIgrK\n1CVlg+Cz+IPWRX6S9rP6QNRQT+rCfxg75cS3e2LnO99e+YYltX/eTpPsMzB