7B7lb/F0bVArMiyd/bwHSzCRfrxspSed8jj\n7Od4GAc4nnPhSKB4dD3ytnRBSGucj5IDPJLY7vdmPsoVJXByNdj3OjqR2BB5\npIy3dXwg1+TTI9wEAZSe4yWPf4oc4NENR/2Wvs6VlgduMEJQEL3gj7rsJDr/\ngYoNNg29+RGXIeJ+l32f65CYROg2b6zao4uhIvzpoRsg5wNBq26U5jm0Gi9Z\nEgeXsn8cByzm6SEc5BZpOkyUSG4q2T6EA3IRUjg+DySf/TBfIpKPDqZynC+h\nxlOUW1h3VOvhoFQORDvIVjjKBDs7G2OjoyIizsf5Es/jcTkt9aehJkTTDsjp\nzaXUKgdpxsfkNIJaWU4fl3NITECUq5mPMmjenwhxtvPhk3ygt66HdW/orQvy\nSB7QW5tHdCmHoiKu9OCnKPB2ruALD+itjMOmtx76DMhLwRnTrIL7Eyy46wi5\nA5+fbjOc2mPm6INncG5BgkeTI4/OwdN9Ew9k+NENo6JzPsQTJuIvTXClL55P\ndXT95X66/5Omx7/cuTJ0uk7kXlpT+WSCkJoUkw3lcUmu7c4dMrrelBheTE+f\nxrlo+7HmHZKaU+YI1orEQ3a8mNpisKgSAA9IQTKt0+rKP8+VkM1JHNXqpyWQ\nWcGXOO1UsnvtXAGL9Yvi8Ap6kMzEJbBLH/WLYuKA4b5fgkKmRXnTzxMEXK/Y\nk+AyRbzFlMGUOEEUyQ2O69Fx56R86QWNcUiAYpaJT2BEqCzCsCwCCyr6BuVL\nr+BLSEZHYn+eOfUZBX6gt4aEug1clQb0AM9odcqXXkEQ17+0N5+D3XuDnhT/\nQjnRPg+9580KPcLrV7ic9KjNEyb9zx3afhyGr5KBFIuXbgToUQbatGnT9qT2\nP2kS4V671P0NAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC\n\"\" alt=\"Variance ratio. \" width=\"391\" height=\"283\" loading=\"lazy\" /><a target=\"_blank\" href=\"../../images/scrna-casestudy-jupyter/pca-variance.png\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><small>Open image in new tab</small></a><br /><br /><figcaption><span class=\"figcaption-prefix\"><strong>Figure 11</strong>:</span> Variance ratio</figcaption></figure>\n<p>We can see that there is really not much variation explained past component 19. So we might save ourselves a great deal of time and muddied data by focusing on the top <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">20</code> PCs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"neighborhood-graph\">Neighborhood graph</h2>\n<p>We’re still looking at around 20 dimensions at this point. We need to identify how similar a cell is to another cell, across every cell across these dimensions. For this, we will use the k-nearest neighbor (kNN) graph, to identify which cells are close together and which are not. The kNN graph plots connections between cells if their distance (when plotted in this 20 dimensional space!) is amonst the k-th smallest distances from that cell to other cells. This will be crucial for identifying clusters, and is necessary for plotting a UMAP. From <a href=\"https://github.com/lmcinnes/umap\">UMAP developers</a>: “Larger neighbor values will result in more global structure being preserved at the loss of detailed local structure. In general this parameter should often be in the range 5 to 50, with a choice of 10 to 15 being a sensible default”.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"details\" style=\"border: 2px solid #ddd; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title details-title\" id=\"details-working-in-a-group-decision-time-2\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button details\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"details-working-in-a-group-decision-time-2\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"fas fa-info-circle\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Details: Working in a group? Decision-time!</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<p>If you are working in a group, you can now divide up a decision here with one <em>control</em> and the rest varied numbers so that you can compare results throughout the tutorials.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Control\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Number of PCs to use</strong> = <code style=\"color: inherit\">20</code></li>\n<li><strong>Maximum number of neighbours used</strong> = <code style=\"color: inherit\">15</code></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Everyone else: Use the PC variance plot to pick your own PC number, and choose your own neighbour maximum as well!</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-55",
      "source": [
        "neighbours = sc.pp.neighbors(pca_components, n_neighbors=15, use_rep='X_pca', n_pcs=20, copy=True)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-56",
      "source": "<h2 id=\"dimensionality-reduction-for-visualisation\">Dimensionality reduction for visualisation</h2>\n<p>Two major visualisations for this data are tSNE and UMAP. We must calculate the coordinates for both prior to visualisation. For tSNE, the parameter <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-019-13056-x\">perplexity</a> can be changed to best represent the data, while for UMAP the main change would be to change the kNN graph above itself, by changing the <b>neighbours.</b></p>\n<blockquote class=\"details\" style=\"border: 2px solid #ddd; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title details-title\" id=\"details-working-in-a-group-decision-time-3\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button details\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"details-working-in-a-group-decision-time-3\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"fas fa-info-circle\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Details: Working in a group? Decision-time!</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<p>If you are working in a group, you can now divide up a decision here with one <em>control</em> and the rest varied numbers so that you can compare results throughout the tutorials.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Control\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Perplexity</strong> = <code style=\"color: inherit\">30</code></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Everyone else: Choose your own perplexity, between 5 and 50!</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-57",
      "source": [
        "tsne_components = sc.tl.tsne(neighbours, use_rep='X_pca', perplexity=30, copy=True)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-58",
      "source": "\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-59",
      "source": [
        "umap_components = sc.tl.umap(tsne_components, copy=True)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-60",
      "source": "<p>{% icon congratulations %} Congratulations! You have prepared your object and created neighborhood coordinates. We can now use those to call some clusters!</p>\n<h1 id=\"cell-clusters--gene-markers\">Cell clusters &amp; gene markers</h1>\n<blockquote class=\"question\" style=\"border: 2px solid #8A9AD0; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title question-title\" id=\"question-4\"><i class=\"far fa-question-circle\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> Question</div>\n<p>Let’s take a step back here. What is it, exactly, that you are trying to get from your data? What do you want to visualise, and what information do you need from your data to gain insight?</p>\n<br/><details style=\"border: 2px solid #B8C3EA; margin: 1em 0.2em;padding: 0.5em; cursor: pointer;\"><summary>👁 View solution</summary>\n<div class=\"box-title solution-title\" id=\"solution-9\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button solution\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"solution-9\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"far fa-eye\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Solution</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<p>Really we need two things - firstly, we need to make sure our experiment was set up well. This is to say, our biological replicates should overlap and our variables should, ideally, show some difference. Secondly, we want insight - we want to know which cell types are in our data, which genes drive those cell types, and in this case, how they might be affected by our biological variable of growth restriction. How does this affect the developing cells, and what genes drive this? So let’s add in information about cell clusters and gene markers!</p>\n</details>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Finally, let’s identify clusters! Unfortunately, it’s not as majestic as biologists often think - the maths doesn’t necessarily identify true cell clusters. Every algorithm for identifying cell clusters falls short of a biologist knowing their data, knowing what cells should be there, and proving it in the lab. Sigh. So, we’re going to make the best of it as a starting point and see what happens! We will define clusters from the kNN graph, based on how many connections cells have with one another. Roughly, this will depend on a resolution parameter for how granular you want to be.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"details\" style=\"border: 2px solid #ddd; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title details-title\" id=\"details-working-in-a-group-decision-time-4\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button details\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"details-working-in-a-group-decision-time-4\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"fas fa-info-circle\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Details: Working in a group? Decision-time!</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<p>Oh yes, yet another decision! Single cell analysis is sadly not straight forward.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Control\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Resolution, high value for more and smaller clusters</strong> = <code style=\"color: inherit\">0.6</code></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Everyone else: Pick your own number. If it helps, this sample should have a lot of very similar cells in it. It contains developing T-cells, so you aren’t expecting massive differences between cells, like you would in, say, an entire embryo, with all sorts of unrelated cell types.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-61",
      "source": [
        "# Find Clusters\n",
        "clusters = sc.tl.louvain(umap_components, resolution=0.6, copy=True)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-62",
      "source": "<p>Nearly plotting time! But one final piece is to add in SOME gene information. Let’s focus on genes driving the clusters.</p>\n<h1 id=\"findmarkers\">FindMarkers</h1>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-63",
      "source": [
        "markers_cluster = sc.tl.rank_genes_groups(clusters, groupby=\"louvain\", method='t-test_overestim_var', n_genes=50, copy=True)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-64",
      "source": "<p>But we are also interested in differences across genotype, so let’s also check that (note that in this case, it’s turning it almost into bulk RNA-seq, because you’re comparing all cells of a certain genotype against all cells of the other)</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-65",
      "source": [
        "markers_genotype = sc.tl.rank_genes_groups(markers_cluster, groupby=\"genotype\", method='t-test_overestim_var', n_genes=50, copy=True)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-66",
      "source": "<p><strong>Note:</strong> The function <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">rank_genes_groups</code> does not return a DataFrame that we can use but instead metadata about the marker table, so first we need to construct the marker table using this generated metadata. This is done using the following function, however it’s not too important to understand what this code does!</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-67",
      "source": [
        "def generate_marker_table(adata):\n",
        "    # extract marker table metadata\n",
        "    res = adata.uns['rank_genes_groups']\n",
        "\n",
        "    # generate DataFrame from metadata\n",
        "    res_df = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "                \"genes\": pd.DataFrame(res[\"names\"]).stack(),\n",
        "                \"scores\": pd.DataFrame(res[\"scores\"]).stack(),\n",
        "                \"logfoldchanges\": pd.DataFrame(res[\"logfoldchanges\"]).stack(),\n",
        "                \"pvals\": pd.DataFrame(res[\"pvals\"]).stack(),\n",
        "                \"pvals_adj\": pd.DataFrame(res[\"pvals_adj\"]).stack(),\n",
        "            })\n",
        "\n",
        "    # convert row names to columns\n",
        "    res_df.index.name = 'newhead'\n",
        "    res_df.reset_index(inplace=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "    # rename generic column names\n",
        "    res_df = res_df.rename(columns={'level_0': 'rank', 'level_1':'cluster'})\n",
        "\n",
        "    # reorder columns\n",
        "    res_df = res_df.reindex(columns=['cluster', 'rank', 'genes', 'scores', 'logfoldchanges', 'pvals', 'pvals_adj'])\n",
        "\n",
        "    # insert ref column\n",
        "    res_df.insert(2, 'ref', 'rest')\n",
        "\n",
        "    return res_df"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-68",
      "source": "<p>Now we can generate our marker tables!</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-69",
      "source": [
        "# Generate marker tables\n",
        "cluster_marker_table = generate_marker_table(markers_cluster)\n",
        "genotype_marker_table = generate_marker_table(markers_genotype)\n",
        "\n",
        "display(cluster_marker_table.head(4))\n",
        "display(genotype_marker_table.head(4))"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-70",
      "source": "<p>Now, there’s a small problem here, which is that if you inspect the output marker tables (above tables), you won’t see gene names, you’ll see Ensembl IDs. While this is a more bioinformatically accurate way of doing this (not every ID has a gene name!), we might want to look at more well-recognised gene names, so let’s pop some of that information in!</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-71",
      "source": [
        "# Join two datasets\n",
        "\n",
        "cluster_joined = pd.merge(cluster_marker_table, markers_cluster.var, left_on='genes', right_on='ID')\n",
        "genotype_joined = pd.merge(genotype_marker_table, markers_genotype.var, left_on='genes', right_on='ID')\n",
        "\n",
        "display(cluster_joined.head(5))\n",
        "display(genotype_joined.head(5))"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-72",
      "source": "\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-73",
      "source": [
        "# Cut columns from tables\n",
        "\n",
        "cluster_markers_named = cluster_joined[['cluster', 'ref', 'rank', 'genes', 'Symbol', 'scores', 'logfoldchanges', 'pvals', 'pvals_adj']]\n",
        "genotype_markers_named = genotype_joined[['cluster', 'ref', 'rank', 'genes', 'Symbol', 'scores', 'logfoldchanges', 'pvals', 'pvals_adj']]\n",
        "\n",
        "display(cluster_markers_named.head(5))\n",
        "display(genotype_markers_named.head(5))"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-74",
      "source": "<p>Well done! It’s time for the best bit, the plotting!</p>\n<h1 id=\"plotting\">Plotting!</h1>\n<p>It’s time! Let’s plot it all! But first, let’s pick some marker genes from the <code class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\">markers_cluster</code> list that you made as well. I’ll be honest, in practice, you’d now be spending a lot of time looking up what each gene does (thank you google!). There are burgeoning automated-annotation tools, however, so long as you have a good reference (a well annotated dataset that you’ll use as the ideal). In the mean time, let’s do this the old-fashioned way, and just copy a bunch of the markers in the original paper.</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-75",
      "source": [
        "# PCA\n",
        "sc.pl.embedding(\n",
        "    markers_cluster,\n",
        "    basis='pca',\n",
        "    color=['louvain','sex','batch','genotype','Il2ra','Cd8b1','Cd8a','Cd4','Itm2a','Aif1','log1p_total_counts'],\n",
        "    gene_symbols='Symbol',\n",
        "    use_raw=False,\n",
        "    save='.png'\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-76",
      "source": "\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-77",
      "source": [
        "# TSNE\n",
        "sc.pl.embedding(\n",
        "    markers_cluster,\n",
        "    basis='tsne',\n",
        "    color=['louvain','sex','batch','genotype','Il2ra','Cd8b1','Cd8a','Cd4','Itm2a','Aif1','log1p_total_counts'],\n",
        "    gene_symbols='Symbol',\n",
        "    use_raw=False,\n",
        "    save='.png'\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-78",
      "source": "\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-79",
      "source": [
        "# UMAP\n",
        "sc.pl.embedding(\n",
        "    markers_cluster,\n",
        "    basis='umap',\n",
        "    color=['louvain','sex','batch','genotype','Il2ra','Cd8b1','Cd8a','Cd4','Itm2a','Aif1','log1p_total_counts'],\n",
        "    gene_symbols='Symbol',\n",
        "    use_raw=False,\n",
        "    save='.png'\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Install libraries"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-80",
      "source": "<p>{% icon congratulations %} Congratulations! You now have plots galore!</p>\n<h1 id=\"insights-into-the-beyond\">Insights into the beyond</h1>\n<p>Now it’s the fun bit! We can see where genes are expressed, and start considering and interpreting the biology of it. At this point, it’s really about what information you want to get from your data - the following is only the tip of the iceberg. However, a brief exploration is good, because it may help give you ideas going forward with for your own data. Let us start interrogating our data!</p>\n<h2 id=\"biological-interpretation\">Biological Interpretation</h2>\n<blockquote class=\"question\" style=\"border: 2px solid #8A9AD0; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title question-title\" id=\"question-appearance-is-everything\"><i class=\"far fa-question-circle\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> Question: Appearance is everything</div>\n<p>Which visualisation is the most useful for getting an overview of our data, <em>pca</em>, <em>tsne</em>, or <em>umap</em>?</p>\n<figure id=\"figure-12\" style=\"max-width: 90%;\"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAADOgAAATSCAMAAACJw8pVAAABxVBMVEX///8k\nerbjd8KUZ73/fw7WJyiNVksfd7QsoCz+/v7/gxY5h70xojH++PhEq0RNk8Mq\nfrj/mDvv9vs4pjjaOTnmhsmQXFL+7+fR4/Bhn8rh7fUwgbqcbGLqmNHurtv3\n+ve10uf//P3T69Pzxub/jCZ1rNGkfsX/p1nYLi34+v2Ds9WmyeGav9zfVlZD\njcDkfMS84but2qz25PM2d63/0armeHjy5OP68vmOu9nq9urg8eBhuGFPsE+X\nbL9YtFjE2eu5m9N9fHtXmcf20tHx+fHup6cTExNrvGuxiX79sXH76vZ0wHRs\npc7m3PDQuLKi0599xH331u3NuN+GyIabcsHz7+/DpqDl3NuPzI/ZyufcR0e4\nlo/jeCCX0JfH5saZaK3/u4FhcLj/xJHoVSSujMz3ehaTlpH/5tD/3sHiZmaZ\nWzyvRDTFqtamfnHLeGnrlpXpioqucI5IkTDzwsHgz8y5urrR0dLxt7fIhLaC\na7vZxsK1YEFmc47GOzXml2JvYGXufjBOfJSJmsbvvJzviErFaS2mp6eZe1qa\nWXWEcSw5OTnde0FukEaSYpRPT0+zeFBljLjFfTxkei6FgsN5o2fVoI+0u9uA\n8DaEAAAACXBIWXMAAC5uAAAubgGOtBeMAAAgAElEQVR42uydz08bWdetnZ50\nShgTxUKCIDAYMSBE8Y8BYGw5AlltCUeykcXFE3vI6Jvcqwv6dCfMGKArRvcP\nvmufc6pc/gFJ+k26TXie7rcDdrngNanirLP2XjuTAQAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA\nAAAAAAAA