QvAl2YQa9yrMZE84ohRkw47oZeUGjyWZcRtA/mNF0\n1orhkN1Rw2FqDu2ok11szthupSfYjjF4gRk5ZnAQod2x7jIJIhkzjImo+3SV\nP+H38gszWuciwjE9JTADZlw3wxTWZs24iKB/MaPxrB3Yx/5ocuDs7KT86cAR\nlMxRH6VfXbtlDF5gxnUzkiBCtekvHESyZhgTUVXXHu8p16OrLs2gLfbX8/H2\nGGbAjBwzTGFt1oyLCPoXMxrPWjE6BFRSvRdnc/r8SkLm7D+yxPb2yRi8wIzr\nZiRBRAV8QDt0smYYE1GFdIaUzjGW38ygQv3z/3AMFMyAGdfNMIW1WTMuIuhf\nzGg+a+npWKADNsyAGb+p1YIHMANm1NoMzNSDIAiCILD2fKXdD01w22AGzIAZ\nv2v56Qa8gBlPNqM+rG3tP7TAkwIzYAbM+F2DTzfgBcx4shnYQ4YgCIIgsBaC\nIAiCwFoIgiAIgsBaCIIgCAJrIQiCIAishSAIgiAIrIUgCIIgsBaCIAiCwFoI\ngiAIgsBaCIIgCAJrIQiCIAgS4vUfG+ZnogCeeTkAAAAASUVORK5CYII=\n\"\" alt=\"FeaturePlot colored by counts. \" width=\"1899\" height=\"617\" loading=\"lazy\" /><a target=\"_blank\" href=\"../../images/scrna-SeuratRStudio/plot18.png\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><small>Open image in new tab</small></a><br /><br /><figcaption><span class=\"figcaption-prefix\"><strong>Figure 20</strong>:</span> FeaturePlot colored by counts split by Individual</figcaption></figure>\n<p>There we go! This might explain the dramatic shift in early to middle T-Cell between wildtype and knockout cells–the leftmost early to middle T-cells simply have a higher sequencing depth represented by Individual 3 (UMIs/cell) than the ones on the right side. Well, that explains some of the sub-cluster that we’re seeing in that area (more specifically, this may account for the discernment between clusters 1, 2, and 5).</p>\n<p>Luckily, and importantly, we don’t see the double negative or mature T-cells being similarly affected. So, although, this variable of sequencing depth, or moreso, Individual, might be something to regress out, it doesn’t seem to be impacting our dataset such that we cannot draw meaningful insights.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\" style=\"border: 2px solid #FFE19E; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title tip-title\" id=\"tip-overprocessing\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button tip\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"tip-overprocessing\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"far fa-lightbulb\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Tip: Overprocessing</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<p>The less you can regress/modify your data, in general, the better–you want to stay as true as you can to the raw data, and only use math to correct your data when you really need to (and not to create insights where there are none!).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Do you think we processed these samples well enough? We have seen in the previous images that these clusters are not very distinct, so we could consider stronger filtering. Let’s take a look at gene expression of a gene we know should not be expressed in tCells as a sanity check:</p>\n",
