  "metadata": {},
  "nbformat": 4,
  "nbformat_minor": 5,
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "metadata",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "<div style=\"border: 2px solid #8A9AD0; margin: 1em 0.2em; padding: 0.5em;\">\n\n# Filter, plot, and explore single cell RNA-seq data with Seurat (R)\n\nby [Camila Goclowski](https://training.galaxyproject.org/hall-of-fame/Camila-goclowski/)\n\nCC-BY licensed content from the [Galaxy Training Network](https://training.galaxyproject.org/)\n\n**Objectives**\n\n- Is my single cell dataset a quality dataset?\n- How do I pick thresholds and parameters in my analysis? What’s a “reasonable” number, and will the world collapse if I pick the wrong one?\n- How do I generate and annotate cell clusters?\n\n**Objectives**\n\n- Interpret quality control plots to direct parameter decisions\n- Repeat analysis from matrix to clustering to labelling clusters\n- Identify decision-making points\n- Appraise data outputs and decisions\n- Explain why single cell analysis is an iterative process (i.e. the first plots you generate are not final, but rather you go back and re-analyse your data repeatedly)\n\n**Time Estimation: 3H**\n</div>\n",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-0",
      "source": "<h1 id=\"setting-your-environment\">Setting your environment</h1>\n<p>First thing’s first, we need to load the packages we will be using. In order to use any functions within a package, we must first call the library of that package. In your console (likely in the lower left corner of your RStudio window), run the following lines of code to do so:</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-1",
      "source": [
        "library(Matrix)\n",
        "library(Seurat)\n",
        "library(dplyr)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-2",
      "source": "<p>The packages are called–now let’s get our data files moved from our Galaxy history and into the RStudio environment so that we can create a Seurat object.</p>\n<h1 id=\"upload-view-and-modify-the-files\">Upload, view and modify the files</h1>\n<p>Now that we’ve made it into RStudio and called the packages we’ll use, let’s begin moving datasets from Galaxy and into RStudio. Galaxy helps us with this by providing the gx_get() function, which will tell us the file paths for the datasets currently occupying our Galaxy histories.</p>\n<p>So, for example, our matrix was the first piece of data to be imported. As such, we will ask for the file path of the first piece of data in our history: we will tell gx_get() the number of the dataset in our history that we are interested in, and the function will output the path we need to bring that dataset into R.</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-3",
      "source": [
        "gx_get(1)  #get the file path for matrix.mtx: #1 in Galaxy history"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-4",
      "source": "<p>Now we have the file path! We can use the Matrix package, specifically the readMM() function, to read the counts matrix into our environment, using the file path above to let it know where to find the matrix.</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-5",
      "source": [
        "matrix.mtx<-readMM(\"/import/1\")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-6",
      "source": "<p>Now we will do the same thing with the feature, barcode, and experimental design files.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"warning\" style=\"border: 2px solid #de8875; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title warning-title\" id=\"warning-don-t-skip-ahead\"><i class=\"fas fa-exclamation-triangle\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> Warning: Don't skip ahead!</div>\n<p>Don’t try to skip ahead and simply fill in the position of the dataset within the readMM() function, reading in the files very often will not work without having used the gx_get() function–if it does, well, lucky you.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>We’ll run the same command, this time replacing the data number in order to ask for the file path to data in an alternative slot in our Galaxy history. Then, we will use the read.delim() function in read in the list of genes, cells, and cell annotations provided to us  by the researchers:</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-7",
      "source": [
        "gx_get(2) #ask for the file path of the genes.tsv file\n",
        "genes.tsv<-read.delim(\"/import/2\", header = FALSE) #read in the genes file\n",
        "\n",
        "gx_get(3) # ask for the file path of the barcodes.tsv file\n",
        "barcodes.tsv<-read.delim(\"/import/3\", header = FALSE) #read in the barcodes file\n",
        "\n",
        "gx_get(4) #ask for the file path of the exp_design.tsv file\n",
        "exp_design.tsv<-read.delim(\"/import/4\") # read in the experimental design file"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-8",
      "source": "<p>The format of the experimental design dataset contains cell barcodes as the first column of data with simply numbered (1-n) row names. In order for Seurat to properly use this dataset, we will need to make the cell barcodes the row names. This can be accomplished by running the following line of code:</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-9",
      "source": [
        "rownames(exp_design.tsv)<-exp_design.tsv$Assay"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-10",
      "source": "<p>Now, in RStudio, we should have all of the data necessary to create a Seurat Object: the matrix, a file with feature (gene) names, a file with cell barcodes, and an optional, but highly useful, experimental design file containing sample (cell-level) metadata.</p>\n<h1 id=\"generating-a-seurat-object\">Generating a Seurat object</h1>\n<p>Next we will add row and column names to our matrix. In the end, this will leave us with a matrix whose rows are genes, columns are cell barcodes, and each value in the matrix representing the expression value of a given gene in a given cell.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\" style=\"border: 2px solid #FFE19E; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title tip-title\" id=\"tip-about-seurat-objects\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button tip\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"tip-about-seurat-objects\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"far fa-lightbulb\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Tip: About Seurat Objects</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<p>The order of the dimensions, such that genes are the first and cells are the second is a set characteristic of Seurat objects.</p>\n<p>You will recieve an error if you try to label the first dimension with cell barcodes or the second with genes. This is because the Dimname slots are like empty fill in the blanks: if the number of labels input doesn’t match the number of blanks in that dimension (the number of labels don’t match the number of cells/genes), Seurat will not accept the labels.</p>\n</blockquote>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-11",
      "source": [
        "dimnames(matrix.mtx) <- list(genes.tsv$V2, barcodes.tsv$V1)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-12",
      "source": "<p>In a more typical Seurat pipeline, or on a local version of RStudio, this step would be replaced with a Read10x() step. Read10x() is Seurat’s function to create a matrix and add in feature and barcode names simultaneously. However, due to the nature of how Galaxy histories and interactive environments communicate with one another, we’ll use this manual method.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\" style=\"border: 2px solid #FFE19E; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title tip-title\" id=\"tip-how-to-use-read10x\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button tip\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"tip-how-to-use-read10x\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"far fa-lightbulb\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Tip: How to Use Read10X</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<p>The only necessary parameter of Seurat’s Read10X() fucntion is the file path to the directory (folder) containing the matrix.mtx, barcodes.tsv, and genes.tsv (sometimes also called features.tsv) files.</p>\n<p>So if the EBI SCXA Retrieval tool were to have output a data directory in the sixth history position, the following code would be able to import a labelled counts matrix called “labelled matrix” into our RStudio environment:</p>\n<div class=\"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge\"><div><pre style=\"color: inherit; background: transparent\"><code style=\"color: inherit\">gx_get(6) #get the file path\nlabelled_matrix&lt;-Read10X(dir = \"/import/6\")\n</code></pre></div>  </div>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now we will create a Seurat object using our newly labelled counts matrix!</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-13",
      "source": [
        "srt<-CreateSeuratObject(counts = matrix.mtx)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-14",
      "source": "<p>You’ve created a Seurat object, congratulations!</p>\n<h1 id=\"adding-cell-level-metadata\">Adding Cell Level Metadata</h1>\n<p>Now that we have an object, we can add in the cell level metadata from our experimental design dataframe (table). This will be useful to us shortly as we begin to visualize the data!</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-15",
      "source": [
        "srt$Sex<-exp_design.tsv$Sample.Characteristic.sex.\n",
        "srt$Organism<-exp_design.tsv$Sample.Characteristic.organism.\n",
        "srt$Strain<-exp_design.tsv$Sample.Characteristic.strain.\n",
        "srt$Developmental.Stage<-exp_design.tsv$Sample.Characteristic.developmental.stage.\n",
        "srt$Age<-exp_design.tsv$Sample.Characteristic.age.\n",
        "srt$Individual<-exp_design.tsv$Sample.Characteristic.individual.\n",
        "srt$Disease<-exp_design.tsv$Sample.Characteristic.disease.\n",
        "srt$Genotype<-exp_design.tsv$Sample.Characteristic.genotype.\n",
        "srt$Organism.Part<-exp_design.tsv$Sample.Characteristic.organism.part.\n",
        "srt$Cell.Type<-exp_design.tsv$Sample.Characteristic.cell.type.\n",
        "srt$Factor.Value.Genotype<-exp_design.tsv$Factor.Value.genotype."
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-16",
      "source": "<blockquote class=\"tip\" style=\"border: 2px solid #FFE19E; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title tip-title\" id=\"tip-syntax-lesson\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button tip\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"tip-syntax-lesson\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"far fa-lightbulb\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Tip: Syntax Lesson</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<p>The code preceding the left pointing arrow will indicate where to put your metadata (the name of your new metadata column: object&#36;new_metadata_columnname), and the code following the arrow will denote where to find that metadata information (metadata_table&#36;columnname)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now that we have our almost fully annotated object, we will add one more metadata column: percent mitochondrial (perc.mt). This metadata column will denote what percentage of a cell’s feature (gene) expression is mitochondrial–which will be useful to us shortly as we begin to filter cells.</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-17",
      "source": [
        "srt <- PercentageFeatureSet(srt, pattern = \"^mt-\", col.name = \"perc.mt\")"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-18",
      "source": "<p>For the sake of this data set, and many others, the mitochondrial genes will all be marked with an “mt” as the prefix, so that is how we have asked Seurat’s PercentageFeatureSet() function to search for mitochondrial genes in the line of code above. With that being said, when you are analyzing your own data, it is highly recommended that you identify how your data set has labelled mitochondrial genes to ensure that you are calculating the correct percentages.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"warning\" style=\"border: 2px solid #de8875; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title warning-title\" id=\"warning-careful\"><i class=\"fas fa-exclamation-triangle\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> Warning: Careful</div>\n<p>The “mt” prefix may not always include all mitochondrial genes depending on how your dataset has been labelled.</p>\n<p>Sometimes the labels may be done via a capital prefix (“^MT”) and in some cases, mitochondrial genes must be identified via feature-level metadata like a GTF file.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h1 id=\"qc-plots\">QC Plots</h1>\n<p>Now that we have a complete Seurat object, we can begin the filtering process.</p>\n<p>There will be a number of ‘cells’ that are low-quality or just empty droplets. There will also be genes which are mere sequencing artifacts or appear with such low frequency that statistical tools will fail to accurately analyse them.</p>\n<p>This background noise of cells and genes not only makes it harder to distinguish real biological information from artifacts, but also makes the data computationally demanding to analyze.</p>\n<p>We want to filter our cells to only include those containing informative biological information. Before doing so, we need to know what the data distribution looks like. There are a number of subjective decisions to make within scRNA-seq analysis, for instance we need to make informed decisions about where to set our thresholds (more on that soon!).</p>\n<p>We’re going to plot our data a few different ways. Different bioinformaticians might prefer to see the data in different ways, and here we are only generating a few of the plots you can use. Ultimately you need to go with what makes the most sense to you.</p>\n<p>So let’s generate some QC plots. First off, let’s check our dataset for batch effect:</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-19",
      "source": [
        "VlnPlot(srt, group.by = \"Individual\",\n",
        "  features = \"nCount_RNA\",\n",
        "  log = TRUE)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-20",
