      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-33",
      "source": [
        "prop.table(table(srt@meta.data$nFeature_RNA > 1275 | srt@meta.data$nFeature_RNA < 575))"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-34",
      "source": "<h1 id=\"applying-our-thresholds\">Applying our Thresholds</h1>\n<p>Once we are happy with our filtering thresholds, it’s time to apply them to our data!</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\" style=\"border: 2px solid #FFE19E; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title tip-title\" id=\"tip-create-a-new-object\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button tip\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"tip-create-a-new-object\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"far fa-lightbulb\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Tip: Create a new object</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<p>You will notice in the next line of code, we have indicated a new object name for this filtered (subset) data. This is good practice  so that you don’t have to start all over in case you decide to change your filtering parameters (which you likely will) or if something goes awry.</p>\n</blockquote>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-35",
      "source": [
        "subset_srt<-subset(srt, nCount_RNA > 1750 & nFeature_RNA > 1275 & perc.mt < 3 | nFeature_RNA < 600)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-36",
      "source": "<p>In this step we are also creating a new object (notice the new object name preceding the subset() function you just ran) so that we may compare back and forth between our unfiltered and filtered data set if we please.</p>\n<p>Next, we want to filter out genes that no longer show any expression in the remaining high quality cells present in our dataset. In order to do so we will extract the filtered matrix from our filtered object.</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-37",
      "source": [
        "subset_matrix<-GetAssayData(subset_srt)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-38",
      "source": "<p>Since you’ve removed a whole heap of cells, and the captured genes are sporadic (i.e. a small percentage of the overall transcriptome per cell) this means there are a number of genes in your matrix that are not expressed in any of the cells left in the filtered matrix. Genes that do not appear in any cell, or even in only 1 or 2 cells, may break some analytical tools and will often not be biologically informative. So let’s remove them!</p>\n<p>We can use the filtered matrix we extracted to create a new Seurat object, this time including the argument: min.cells = 3. This will remove any genes from our matrix that have less than 3 cells expressing them.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\" style=\"border: 2px solid #FFE19E; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title tip-title\" id=\"tip-more-thresholds\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button tip\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"tip-more-thresholds\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"far fa-lightbulb\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Tip: More Thresholds</span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<p>Note that 3 is not necessarily the best number, rather it is a fairly conservative threshold. You could go as high as 10 or more.</p>\n</blockquote>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-39",
      "source": [
        "filtered_srt <- CreateSeuratObject(counts = subset_matrix, meta.data = subset_srt@meta.data, min.cells = 3)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-40",
      "source": "<p>Now that we have filtered out both noisy “cells” and genes from our dataset, let’s clean up our environment. Remove objects that we no longer need to ensure that we stay organized and RStudio has enough memory capacity to perform downstream analyses. This likely will not be an issue while doing this tutorial, but in practice it will help things run smoothly.</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-41",
      "source": [
        "rm(subset_matrix, subset_srt)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-42",
      "source": "<h1 id=\"processing\">Processing</h1>\n<p>Even after filtering, we still have quite big data. This presents two concerns for downstream analyses:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>We already saw in our filtering plots that there are differences in how many transcripts and genes have been counted per cell. This technical variable could, and likely will, distract us from identifying true biological differences.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>We like to plot things on 2-dimensional (X/Y) plots. For instance, Gapdh could be on one axis, and Actin could be on another, and each cell could be plotted onto that 2D axis based on how many of each transcript they possess.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Although this plotting method would be <em>fine</em>, adding in a 3rd dimension (or, indeed, in our case, a dimension for each of the remaining thousands of genes), is a bit trickier.</p>\n<p>So, our next steps will be to transform the large data object into something that is easy to analyse and easy to visualize.</p>\n<p>We will run SCTransform, a combinatorial function by Seurat that normalizes the data, identifies variable features, and then scales the data. In their initial workflow, and in the Scanpy version of this tutorial, these steps are run individually. However, with the second version of SCTransform comes time efficiency and optimization for downstream analyses.</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-43",
      "source": [
        "filtered_srt<- SCTransform(filtered_srt, vars.to.regress = c(\"perc.mt\", \"nFeature_RNA\", \"nCount_RNA\"), verbose = TRUE, return.only.var.genes = FALSE, seed.use = 1448145)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-44",
      "source": "<blockquote class=\"comment\" style=\"border: 2px solid #ffecc1; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title comment-title\" id=\"comment-what-is-normalization\"><i class=\"far fa-comment-dots\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> Comment: What is Normalization?</div>\n<p>Normalisation helps reduce the differences between gene and UMI counts by fitting total counts across cells in our data to be comparable to one another. SCTransform regularizes the gene expression profiles via a negative binomial regression while also controlling for overfitting of the data. This step can also be done using Seurat’s NormalizeData() function, but would need to be followed by FindVariableFeatures() and ScaleData().</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>We also have loads of genes, but not all of them vary in expression from cell to cell. For instance, housekeeping genes are defined as not changing much from cell to cell, so we could disclude these from our data to simplify our analyses.</p>\n<p>The find variable features step within SCTransform (or Seurat’s FindVariableFeatures() function) will flag genes that <em>do</em> vary across cells, expediting future analyses and ensuring that we, and Seurat, don’t waste time looking for meaningful differences where they don’t exist.</p>\n<p>Finally, SCTransform (or Seurat’s ScaleData() function) will scale the data so that all genes have the same variance and a zero mean.</p>\n<p>This is an important step to set up our data for further dimensionality reduction. It also helps negate sequencing depth differences between samples, since the gene levels across the cells become comparable.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"comment\" style=\"border: 2px solid #ffecc1; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title comment-title\" id=\"comment-don-t-worry\"><i class=\"far fa-comment-dots\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> Comment: Don't Worry!</div>\n<p>Note, that the differences from scaling etc. are not the values you have at the end - i.e. if your cell has average GAPDH levels, it will not appear as a ‘0’ when you calculate gene differences between clusters.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Although we’ve made our expression values comparable to one another and our overall dataset less computationally demanding, we still have <em>way</em> too many dimensions (n cells x n genes!).</p>\n<p>Transcript changes are not usually singular–which is to say, genes function and exist in pathways and groups. It would be easier to analyse our data if we could group these differences. To address this we will run principal component analysis (PCA).</p>\n<blockquote class=\"comment\" style=\"border: 2px solid #ffecc1; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title comment-title\" id=\"comment-what-is-pca\"><i class=\"far fa-comment-dots\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> Comment: What is PCA?</div>\n<p>Principal components (PCs) are calculated from high dimensional data to find the most representative spread in the dataset. So in our highly variable gene dimensions, there will be one line (axis) that yields the most spread and variation across the cells. That will be our first principal component.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>We can calculate the first handful of principal components in our data to drastically reduce the number of dimensions:</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\" style=\"border: 2px solid #FFE19E; margin: 1em 0.2em\">\n<div class=\"box-title tip-title\" id=\"tip-running-computationally-demanding-steps-on-variable-features\"><button class=\"gtn-boxify-button tip\" type=\"button\" aria-controls=\"tip-running-computationally-demanding-steps-on-variable-features\" aria-expanded=\"true\"><i class=\"far fa-lightbulb\" aria-hidden=\"true\" ></i> <span>Tip: Running Computationally Demanding Steps on Variable Features </span><span class=\"fold-unfold fa fa-minus-square\"></span></button></div>\n<p>You’ll notice that the RunPCA() function is run using the variable features from the previous step. This signficantly decreases the number of genes, and their expression changes, that must be grouped into principal components by this step.</p>\n</blockquote>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-45",
      "source": [
        "filtered_srt <- RunPCA(filtered_srt, features = VariableFeatures(object = filtered_srt))"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-46",
      "source": "<p>To visualize how our principal components (PCs) represent our data, let’s create an elbow plot:</p>\n",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "editable": false,
        "collapsed": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-47",
      "source": [
        "ElbowPlot(filtered_srt, ndims = 50)"
      ],
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "attributes": {
          "classes": [
            "# Setting your environment"
          ],
          "id": ""
        }
      }
    },
